  "title": "A Regular at Marikiti Market",
  "category": "commerce",
  "difficulty": "advanced",
  "level": 8,
  "order": 801,
  "theme": "market",
  "culturalNote": "Marikiti is Nairobi's huge wholesale fruit and vegetable market. Become a regular and the sellers start to know you — and the prices get better. Habits are where Kenyan Swahili gets colourful: Sanifu uses hu- (\"mimi hununua hapa\" = I usually buy here), while Kenyans love adding -nga, borrowed from Luhya: \"ninanunuanga hapa\", \"huwa nanunuanga\". Deals are made with -ki- (if): \"ukinunua kilo tatu, nitakupunguzia\" (if you buy three kilos, I'll knock the price down for you).",
  "startingPoint": "Saturday morning at Marikiti. You walk up to the stall of Mama Wanjiru, whom you've bought from before.",
  "grammarFocus": [
    "habitual-hu-nga",
    "ki-po-ka",
